    "body_text": "Tequila and mezcal are Mexican agave spirits, but the names describe different regulated products. Knowing a little about each helps when you read a New York cocktail menu or choose a bottle to explore.\n\nStart with the agave\n\nTequila is made from blue Weber agave within its authorized production regions in Mexico. Mezcal can use different permitted agave varieties and has its own production rules. The label's producer, origin and agave information can tell you more than a broad description such as smooth or smoky.\n\nTaste beyond one stereotype\n\nSome mezcals have a noticeable roasted or smoky character, but smoke is not a complete definition of mezcal. Tequila also varies: agave, fermentation, distillation and maturation all affect the result. Look for fruit, herbs, earth, spice and texture as well as smoke or oak.\n\nChoose for the cocktail\n\nA citrus-led drink may suit a bright agave profile, while a stirred drink can highlight richer flavours. Swapping tequila for mezcal changes the balance; it is not simply a stronger version of the same recipe. Ask about the particular spirit rather than assuming every bottle tastes alike.\n\nExplore the NYC menu\n\nSilent H's regional cocktails offer one way to discover agave flavours. Aitch, our upcoming tequila bar next door, has a separate drinks menu. Browse the menus before opening to find a starting point that matches your tastes.\n\nSilent H NYC opening updates\n\nSilent H is opening soon at 420 West 13th Street in the Meatpacking District. Aitch will be next door at 418 West 13th Street. Reservations are coming soon. Follow our NYC opening updates before planning a visit.",
